<body>
  <main>
    <header>
      <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Everything you need to know about cats</p>
    </header>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <div class="example">
        <h3>
          <button type="button" aria-expanded="false">
            Why do cats purr?
            <span class="toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
        </h3>
        <div class="details hidden">
          <p>Cats purr for many reasons — contentment, self-soothing, and healing. Purring is a complex behavior triggered by emotions and physical states. Mother cats purr to guide kittens, while adult cats purr when relaxed or seeking attention. Interestingly, cats also purr when injured or stressed, suggesting it may have evolved as a self-healing mechanism.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <h3>
          <button type="button" aria-expanded="false">
            How many hours do cats sleep?
            <span class="toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
        </h3>
        <div class="details hidden">
          <p>Cats are champion sleepers, spending 12-16 hours per day sleeping or in a resting state. This is a remnant of their wild ancestry — in the wild, cats conserve energy through extended sleep, allowing them to hunt during peak activity times. Kittens and senior cats may sleep even more, up to 20 hours daily.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <h3>
          <button type="button" aria-expanded="false">
            What do cats' different meows mean?
            <span class="toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
        </h3>
        <div class="details hidden">
          <p>Cats have a wide range of vocalizations, each with its own meaning. Short meows are greetings, long meows express complaints or demands, and rapid-fire meows show excitement. Chirps and trills indicate friendly communication, while hisses and growls are clear warnings. Interestingly, cats rarely meow at each other — they reserve this behavior mainly for communicating with humans.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <h3>
          <button type="button" aria-expanded="false">
            Why do cats have a third eyelid?
            <span class="toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
        </h3>
        <div class="details hidden">
          <p>The third eyelid, called the nictitating membrane, is a protective covering that helps keep a cat's eyes moist and clean. It sweeps across the eye to remove debris and protective secretions. If a cat's third eyelid becomes visible, it can be a sign of illness or stress, so it's worth consulting a veterinarian if you notice this change.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <h3>
          <button type="button" aria-expanded="false">
            How do cats land on their feet?
            <span class="toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
        </h3>
        <div class="details hidden">
          <p>Cats possess an incredible righting reflex that allows them to rotate their bodies mid-air to land on their feet. This ability relies on their flexible spine, keen inner ear balance system, and visual processing. Starting from just a few feet, kittens gradually develop this skill, which becomes nearly automatic by adulthood. However, falls from extreme heights can still cause serious injury.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <h3>
          <button type="button" aria-expanded="false">
            Can cats see in the dark?
            <span class="toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
        </h3>
        <div class="details hidden">
          <p>Cats have superior night vision compared to humans, thanks to a high concentration of rod cells in their retinas and a reflective layer called the tapetum lucidum. This layer acts like a mirror, bouncing light back through the retina to increase sensitivity. While cats can't see in complete darkness, they need only about one-sixth the light that humans require to see clearly.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <h3>
          <button type="button" aria-expanded="false">
            How far can a cat jump?
            <span class="toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
        </h3>
        <div class="details hidden">
          <p>Cats are incredibly athletic and can jump up to six times their body length horizontally and eight times their body length vertically. This remarkable jumping ability comes from their powerful hind legs, flexible spine, and excellent depth perception. A typical cat can leap onto a 6-foot fence with ease, making them impressive acrobats in the animal world.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="example">
        <h3>
          <button type="button" aria-expanded="false">
            Why do cats knock things off tables?
            <span class="toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
        </h3>
        <div class="details hidden">
          <p>Cats knock things off surfaces for several reasons: curiosity about how objects behave, testing their hunting and pouncing skills, or seeking attention from their owners. Some behavioral experts believe it's also a way for cats to mark territory or create an enriched environment. While frustrating for humans, this behavior is completely normal feline activity.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <script>
    // Initialize disclosure widgets
    document.querySelectorAll('button[aria-expanded]').forEach((btn) => {
      const panel = btn.closest('h3').nextElementSibling;

      // Hide panels on init — content stays visible without JS (reader mode, no-JS)
      panel.hidden = true;
      panel.classList.add('hidden');

      btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        const isOpen = btn.getAttribute('aria-expanded') === 'true';
        btn.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(!isOpen));
        panel.hidden = isOpen;
        panel.classList.toggle('hidden', isOpen);
      });

      // Optional: Allow Escape key to close
      btn.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
        if (e.key === 'Escape' && btn.getAttribute('aria-expanded') === 'true') {
          btn.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'false');
          panel.hidden = true;
          panel.classList.add('hidden');
          btn.focus();
        }
      });
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
